      'Blaise Pascal was born on 19 June 1623 in Clermont, in central France. His father, Étienne, was a civil servant with a keen interest in mathematics, and educated his children at home because Blaise was often ill. The family moved to Paris and later to Rouen, where Étienne was a tax official. At sixteen, Blaise wrote a short essay on conic sections that included a result now known as Pascal’s theorem. A few years later, to help his father with the endless sums of tax work, he built a mechanical calculator, now known as the Pascaline.',
