      'Baruch Spinoza (in Portuguese Bento, in Latin Benedictus) was born in Amsterdam in 1632 into a family of Portuguese Jewish origin. His family’s ancestors had fled Iberia, where Jews had been forced to convert or leave, and had found refuge in the tolerant Dutch Republic. He studied at the community school, but left formal studies in his teens to help with the family import business. He also read widely outside the Jewish tradition, studying Latin and the philosophy of Descartes and mixing with Christian free-thinkers.',
      'On 27 July 1656 the leaders of Amsterdam’s Sephardic Jewish community issued a harsh writ of excommunication (herem) against him, citing “abominable heresies” and “monstrous deeds”, though the exact reasons are not spelled out. The ban was never lifted. Spinoza never married, left Amsterdam, and made his living grinding optical lenses, which brought him into contact with scientists such as Christiaan Huygens. He moved to Rijnsburg around 1661, later to Voorburg, and finally to The Hague, where he lived modestly in rented rooms.',
      'Only two of his books appeared in his lifetime: an exposition of Descartes’s philosophy (1663), under his own name, and the Theological-Political Treatise (1670), which was published anonymously and soon denounced by many authorities as dangerous. He declined a professorship at Heidelberg, perhaps because it might limit his freedom to think. He met Leibniz in 1676. He died on 21 February 1677 in The Hague, aged 44, of a lung illness, probably tuberculosis and possibly worsened by glass dust from his work. His friends published his Ethics and other writings later that year.',
