      'Gottfried Wilhelm Leibniz was born on 1 July 1646 in Leipzig, in Germany. His father, a professor of moral philosophy, died when Leibniz was six, and the boy taught himself from the books in his father’s library. He studied at Leipzig and then at Altdorf, where he received a doctorate in law in 1666, at about the age of twenty. Even then he was sketching a plan for a general method of reasoning, in his Dissertation on the Art of Combinations.',
      'He entered the service of the Elector of Mainz, who sent him to Paris on a diplomatic mission, and he stayed there from 1672 to 1676. In Paris he studied mathematics with Christiaan Huygens, met leading philosophers, designed a calculating machine that could multiply and divide, and developed his version of calculus. He also visited London in 1673 and showed his machine to the Royal Society. In 1676 he took a post at the court of Hanover, where he served three successive dukes and electors as librarian, adviser and historian for the rest of his life.',
      'His duties at Hanover included a long, unfinished history of the House of Brunswick, and technical projects such as an attempt to improve mining in the Harz mountains with windmills, which the duke shut down in 1685. He corresponded with over a thousand people, helped to found the Berlin Academy in 1700, and promoted scientific societies across Europe. He never married. In his last years he was embroiled in a bitter dispute with Newton’s supporters over calculus. He died in Hanover on 14 November 1716, and the funeral was attended by very few of the court he had served.',
